<p>Completed in 1915 as Mandan's U.S. post office, the First Street Federal Building followed plans by {% person "james-knox-taylor" %}James Knox Taylor{% endperson %}, FAIA. Taylor served as the {% place "us-treasury-department" %}U.S. Treasury Department's{% endplace %} supervising architect from 1897 through 1912. His successor, {% person "oscar-wenderoth" %}Oscar Wenderoth{% endperson %}, supervised the building's construction and commissioning. A similar but slightly smaller federal building was designed and constructed concurrently at {% place "wahpeton-north-dakota" %}Wahpeton{% endplace %}.</p>

<p>Taylor became known as the “national architect” because of improvements in federal design and construction during his 15-year tenure. By the end of his federal career, the Treasury commissioned an average of ten buildings per month nationwide. Taylor participated in the construction of more than 800 federal buildings, helping create a recognizable federal architectural identity.</p>

<p>Taylor sought to harmonize federal architecture with local history, climate, and environment, specifying North Dakota brick for the building. Although smaller and less ornate than many period federal buildings, its main elevation was carefully proportioned to create a dignified, solid character. Iron lamps, recessed windows, and elegant arches contributed to an effect based on unity, scale, symmetry, rhythm, and classical ornament.</p>

