<p>The {% place "national-geodetic-survey" %}National Geodetic Survey{% endplace %} selected the "massive structure" to hold an elevation benchmark in 1931. The marker was installed near the building's southwestern corner, on the southern wall approximately five feet above ground. A 1982 survey team could not locate it, reportedly because a bush concealed both the marker and cornerstone. Another possible explanation is that the team searched the current post office two blocks away. The database was updated in 2007 to report the marker in good condition.</p>

<p>The marker has three rings of text, from outside to inside:</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>US COAST &amp; GEODETIC SURVEY BENCHMARK<br>
  ELEV 1651.368 FEET ABOVE MEAN SEA LEVEL<br>
  $250 FINE OR IMPRISIONMENT FOR DISTURBING THIS MARKER.</p>
</blockquote>

<p>The building served as Mandan's post office through 1962. Ownership then passed to the {% place "mandan-library-district" %}Mandan Library District{% endplace %}. The library had occupied the {% place "mandan-memorial-building" %}World War Memorial Building's{% endplace %} basement since 1939; that building now serves as City Hall. After remodeling, the former post office housed the library from 1964 through 2003. The {% place "national-register-of-historic-places" %}National Register of Historic Places{% endplace %} listed it as a contributing structure to the {% place "mandan-commercial-historic-district" %}Mandan Commercial Historic District{% endplace %} in 1985. A 2004 move took the collection to the {% place "northern-pacific-freight-house" %}Northern Pacific Freight House{% endplace %} on West Main Street.</p>

<p>The city solicited purchase proposals in 2003, but interest was limited because remediation of the <a href="/area-history/downtown-mandan-petroleum-plume/">downtown petroleum plume</a> had not begun. The vacant building was left unheated, prompting preservation advocates to accuse the city of allowing demolition by neglect. It was razed in January 2008 for additional senior housing.</p>
